1️⃣ What is Event Driven Architecture?

In traditional systems:

Transaction Service → Account Service → Notification Service

This is tight coupling.

In Event Driven Architecture:

Transaction Service
        |
   Publish Event
        |
     Event Broker
        |
   -----------------------
   |         |           |
Notification  Audit   Fraud
 Service     Service  Service

Event broker = Apache Kafka
Event-Driven Architecture (EDA) is a software design pattern where systems communicate and react to events—changes in state or important actions—rather than direct requests.

🔹 Simple idea

Instead of one component directly calling another (like “do this now”), components emit events, and other components listen and respond to them.

🔹 What is an “event”?

An event is any significant occurrence, such as:

A user placing an order

A payment being completed

A file being uploaded

🔹 Core components

Event Producers (Publishers)

Generate events

Example: An e-commerce app creating an “Order Placed” event

Event Consumers (Subscribers)

Listen for and react to events

Example: A shipping service reacting to “Order Placed”

Event Broker / Message Queue

Transfers events between producers and consumers

Examples include systems like Apache Kafka or RabbitMQ

🔹 How it works (flow)

A producer emits an event

The event is sent to a broker

Consumers subscribed to that event receive it

Each consumer processes it independently

🔹 Example

Imagine an online shopping system:

User places an order → OrderCreated event

Multiple services react:

Payment service processes payment

Inventory service updates stock

Notification service sends confirmation

All of this happens without tight coupling between services.

🔹 Key benefits

Loose coupling → services don’t depend directly on each other

Scalability → consumers can scale independently

Flexibility → easy to add new features without changing existing code

Real-time processing → instant reactions to events

🔹 Challenges

Harder to debug (events flow asynchronously)

Event ordering can be tricky

Requires good monitoring and design

🔹 Where it’s used

Microservices architectures

Real-time analytics

IoT systems

Financial transactions

Notification systems
