🔹 Kafka Partitions & Offsets
📌 Partitions

A topic is split into multiple partitions

Each partition is an ordered, immutable log

Enables parallel processing & scalability

👉 More partitions = more consumers can read in parallel

📌 Offsets

Each message in a partition has a unique offset (ID)

Consumers track offsets to know:

What they’ve processed

Where to resume after failure

👉 Offset = position of event in the log

🔹 Exactly-Once Delivery
📌 Problem

Avoid processing the same event multiple times (duplicates)

✅ Kafka Solution

Kafka provides Exactly-Once Semantics (EOS) using:

Idempotent producers (no duplicate writes)

Transactions (write + consume atomically)

👉 Ensures:

No duplicates

No data loss

⚠️ Reality: “Exactly-once” is guaranteed within Kafka, but external systems (DB, APIs) must also be handled carefully.

🔹 Log Compaction
📌 What it is

Kafka can retain only the latest value per key instead of all events.

📌 Example

Key = user123

update1 → update2 → update3

After compaction → only update3 remains

📌 Use cases

State storage (latest user profile)

Caching

Event sourcing snapshots

🔹 Schema Registry (Avro)
📌 What it is

A centralized service to manage event schemas

Commonly used with:

Avro format (compact, binary, schema-based)

👉 Tool: Confluent Schema Registry

📌 Why important

Ensures producers & consumers agree on structure

Prevents breaking changes

Enables schema evolution

🔹 Event Versioning
📌 Problem

Events change over time (new fields, removed fields)

✅ Solutions

Backward compatible changes

Add optional fields

Versioned topics

user.v1, user.v2

Schema evolution

Managed via Schema Registry

👉 Goal: old consumers should still work

🔹 Outbox Pattern
📌 Problem

Keeping DB + Kafka in sync
(e.g., write to DB but fail to publish event)

✅ Solution: Outbox Pattern

Write event to an outbox table in DB

A separate process publishes events to Kafka

📌 Flow

App writes business data + event to DB (same transaction)

Outbox table stores event

Background worker reads outbox

Publishes to Kafka

👉 Guarantees no event loss

🔹 Why it matters (big picture)

All these concepts together solve:

Scalability → partitions

Reliability → retries, DLQ, exactly-once

Data consistency → outbox, transactions

Evolution → schema registry + versioning

Efficiency → log compaction

